Backend Array parameter Why it is safe CPU LLVM pointer without <code>noalias</code> (default variant) LLVM must assume any two pointers may alias CUDA / HIP <code>T* name</code> — no <code>__restrict__</code> <code>CUDACodeGen.generate</code> says it directly: restrict would let the compiler reuse a value across a store through another array Level Zero <code>__global T* name</code> — no <code>restrict</code> <code>OpenCLCodeGen.generate</code> emits plain <code>__global</code> pointers Metal a member of one argument buffer MSL itself forbids overlap between separate buffer arguments; see below <p>Metal is the exception, because MSL forbids overlap by construction. There is no <code>restrict</code> keyword to remove: section 5.2 of the Metal Shading Language specification states that device buffers passed as separate kernel arguments must not overlap. Binding two aliasing arrays to two <code>[[buffer(n)]]</code> slots is outside the language, and on an Apple M1 Max four overlap cases failed numerically (the contract's regression baseline, "Metal validation, 2026-10-03").</p> <p>The fix is indirect binding. <code>MSLCodeGen.generate</code> (<code>codegen/msl_gen.py</code>) emits one struct holding every non-texture array pointer, with <code>[[id(i)]]</code> equal to the parameter's position:</p> <pre><code>struct __tack_buffer_args__ {
    device float* a [[id(0)]];
    device float* b [[id(1)]];
};

kernel void k(constant __tack_buffer_args__&amp; __tack_buffers__ [[buffer(0)]],
              uint __tid__ [[thread_position_in_grid]])
{
    device float* a = __tack_buffers__.a;
    device float* b = __tack_buffers__.b;
    ...
}
</code></pre> <p>The kernel has a single buffer argument, so the separate-argument rule has nothing to apply to, and the array pointers inside it may alias. Textures keep their own <code>[[texture(n)]]</code> namespace; unpacked scalars (seen only when generating source directly) remain <code>constant T&amp;</code> arguments after the argument buffer. Argument buffers are a Metal 2 feature.</p> <p>On the runtime side, <code>_compile_kernel</code> in <code>runtime/metal.py</code> creates an <code>MTLArgumentEncoder</code> with <code>newArgumentEncoderWithBufferIndex_(0)</code>. On every dispatch, <code>CompiledMetalKernel.__call__</code> allocates a fresh shared-storage argument buffer, encodes each array's <code>MTLBuffer</code> into it and declares its residency with <code>useResource_usage_(..., Read | Write)</code> — indirectly referenced resources need that explicit declaration, and it is refreshed each time because a cached variant can receive different buffers, or a different alias relationship, from one call to the next. The argument buffer belongs to the launch, and is kept alive until the launch completes, because launches are queued: one buffer reused across dispatches could be rewritten under a launch that has not run yet. No GPU backend specializes on alias relationships: one variant serves every combination.</p>
